{{Source status|status=V2 operational guidance|note=BITwiki-specific contribution pathway informed by Wikipedia newcomer/help systems but adapted to a source-first human+AI knowledge corpus and the programmable MediaWiki/BIThub substrate.}}

= Contributing to BITwiki =

BITwiki should be easier to improve than it is to misunderstand.

You do not need to create a new article to contribute. In many cases, improving an existing page, adding a source, clarifying a technical explanation, recording a missing topic, fixing navigation, tightening a semantic contract, or updating living architecture text creates more value than adding another shallow page.

== Start here ==

Choose the smallest useful contribution:

{| class="wikitable"
! I want to… !! Best first action
|-
| fix an error || correct the specific statement and preserve/source the reason
|-
| add evidence || improve references, provenance, evidence notes, or source quality
|-
| make a page understandable || improve the Simple/Core entry without deleting needed technical detail
|-
| add technical depth || extend mechanisms, methods, implementation, standards, data, or limitations
|-
| connect knowledge || add justified relationships, categories, portal links, outlines, or cross-domain context
|-
| cover a missing topic || document the gap with supporting sources through an available authorized contribution channel; do not create an empty public article
|-
| create a new page || research, source, model, write, verify, then publish a minimum coherent knowledge object
|-
| improve BITwiki itself || improve its public architecture, navigation, governance, explanations, semantic contracts or contribution guidance
|}

'''Intended request channels:''' readers and collaborators should be able to propose an edit, ask for verification, request new knowledge, submit a Governance RFC or raise a Technical/Schema Request as distinguished in [[BITwiki:Editorial protocol]]. A future request-an-edit interaction is intended to work without ordinary MediaWiki sign-in; no live button, modal, unified intake or finalized permission routing is claimed here. Public suggestions and privileges reserved for authorized members (including paid access where applicable) are distinct, but their precise rights, priorities and enforcement are not established by this document.

== Before creating a new subject page ==

# Search for an existing canonical page, redirect, Book Matter fragment, related page or known coverage request that already performs or tracks the job. Internal records are not public wiki pages.
# Decide whether the material is a durable knowledge object, a navigation surface, project documentation, or a request/backlog item.
# Evaluate the subject's independent intellectual value, evidence, appropriate identity and meaningful connections on their own terms. No discipline needs to prove computational or informational utility to be admissible. See [[BITwiki:Page format]] and [[BITwiki:Editorial protocol]].
# Verify that the canonical title names the actual subject; check established usage and existing pages. Do not rename a legitimate subject to force it into a computational or other fashionable frame.
# Gather strong sources before writing factual claims.
# Choose semantic identity deliberately: Entity type, Domain(s), epistemic status, and provenance must not be guessed by a template default.
# Write enough coherent content that a reader can understand what the subject is and why the page exists.

'''Do not create generic shells merely to reserve titles.''' Do not manufacture a computational metaphor, a token mention of information, or a decorative semantic relationship to fit a general subject into an artificial gate. Review knowledge value, evidence and connections on their merits; source validators and staging builds cannot replace editorial judgment.

A missing page is better represented as an explicit request than as a pseudo-article with one sentence and no evidence.

== Minimum coherent subject page ==

A new canonical subject normally needs enough signal to answer:

* '''What is it?''' — understandable lead and definition.
* '''Why does it matter?''' — the subject's importance and independent educational, scientific, historical or conceptual value. When describing a computational model or measurement method, distinguish it from its target phenomenon.
* '''How does it work or what are its core concepts?'''
* '''What are representative examples/applications?'''
* '''What is uncertain, disputed, contextual, or limited?''' when relevant.
* '''Where did the information come from?''' — traceable references/provenance.

Use [[BITwiki:Page format]] as the grammar, not as a requirement to fill every possible heading.

== Improve before inventing ==

A good newcomer path is often:

<pre>
read
→ notice a gap/error
→ make one sourced improvement
→ learn the corpus structure
→ make larger changes
→ create new knowledge only when needed
</pre>

This reduces duplicate pages, speculative schema, and low-information stubs.

== Simple, Core and Technical; research within the subject ==

Accessibility, technical depth and honest treatment of open questions are all valid contribution targets.

* Improve the '''Simple''' orientation when a knowledgeable but non-specialist reader cannot enter the subject.
* Improve the '''Core''' treatment when conceptual explanation, context, evidence or relationships are missing.
* Improve the '''Technical''' treatment when meaningful formalism, implementation, standards, methods or advanced limitations are absent.
* Improve '''frontier research and open questions''' within the appropriate Core or Technical article. Frontier is an evidentiary/research condition, '''not a fourth required reader-depth page'''.

See [[BITwiki:Readable depth and localization]].

== Reusing existing material ==

When contributing an explanation that appears elsewhere, distinguish a '''link''' (separate subjects), a '''contextual summary''' (local argument), and a '''transclusion''' (the same maintained wording). Do not create a fragment merely because a section has a recognized Book Matter role. Review canonical ownership, scope, citations, semantic annotations and dependent host pages under [[BITwiki:Editorial protocol]]. See [[Help:Transclusion]] for syntax and verification.

== Human + AI contributions ==

AI assistance is allowed as tooling; it does not create authority by itself. Authorized agents may eventually carry out bounded editorial evaluation and decisions with recorded provenance and accountable permissions; not every action must receive separate human approval. This is intended governance behavior, not confirmation of a deployed autonomous workflow.

Human or agent contributions must:

* preserve source attribution and provenance;
* distinguish source material from synthesis and inference;
* not invent references, implementation state, consensus, or semantic identity;
* make uncertainty visible rather than smoothing it away;
* preserve meaningful authored/historical voice where canonicalization requires it;
* remain reviewable and reversible through normal version history.

Sensitive subject matter requires case-specific evaluation under [[BITwiki:Constitution|the Constitution]] and [[BITwiki:Editorial protocol|the editorial protocol]]. Historical research proposals are not binding policy until separately adopted.

== Programmable-substrate contributions ==

BITwiki separates authoring, structured state, computation, and interaction:

<pre>
wikitext / Templates + SMW + Cargo
              ↓
      MediaWiki + Scribunto/Lua
              ↓
 deterministic views / diagnostics
              ↕
 authenticated integration boundary
              ↕
      BIThub / Discourse
</pre>

Choose the layer that owns the change:

* canonical knowledge/prose → Main article; reusable Book Matter stays inline unless extraction is editorially justified;
* semantic graph contract/assertion → Property + SMW/page state;
* repeated operational record → Cargo-backed workflow;
* declarative public invocation → Template;
* non-trivial reusable normalization/type checking/bounded inference/projection → <code>Module:BITwiki/*</code>;
* project architecture/governance → BITwiki namespace;
* end-user workflow guidance → Help namespace;
* cross-service transport → authenticated MediaWiki/Discourse plugin/API/service/agent infrastructure, not Scribunto.

A Lua-derived display or diagnostic is not automatically a new canonical assertion. A BIThub post or agent result is not automatically canonical knowledge.

See [[BITwiki:Programmable knowledge substrate]], [[BITwiki:Lua architecture]], and [[BITwiki:Ecosystem]].

== Navigation contributions ==

A portal or category should exist because it helps readers traverse a real body of knowledge, not merely because a topic name exists.

Before creating one, ask whether a current Portal, Category, Concept, outline, list, query, graph view, Lua projection, or related-links section can do the job.

For categories, explain scope, includes, excludes/boundary, category role, parents/subcategories, important pages, and related navigation.

Navigation projections should consume canonical state rather than become independent semantic databases.

== Proposing missing knowledge ==

First check for a canonical article, redirect, alternate name, relevant section, or another treatment of the subject. A useful proposal identifies the missing question, why existing coverage does not answer it, and the sources or distinctions that could support an article or expansion.

A proposal is not an article. Do not publish empty shells to reserve titles, and do not present an internal research queue as a public knowledge surface. [[BITwiki:Editorial protocol]] distinguishes proposed request types, but it does not assert that a unified public submission interface or an internal workflow engine has been deployed.

Once material is adequately sourced and reviewed, improve the existing article or create a coherent new account with appropriate links and attribution. Do not make repository task statuses part of the article's subject matter.

== Living architecture and repository documentation ==

Architecture work is incomplete when only the implementation or only one specification knows about the change.

When a contribution changes a directory's responsibility, dependency, source-of-truth boundary, deployment order, or connection to another subsystem:

# update the implementation/state;
# update the authoritative [[BITwiki:Organization|BITwiki architecture]] or focused standard where semantics changed;
# update the nearest repository <code>README.md</code> so maintainers encounter the current model locally;
# update <code>manifest.json</code> when source/runtime mapping or architecture metadata changed;
# update validation/audits when the invariant can be machine-checked;
# preserve <code>archive-v1/</code> read-only while issue #152 reconciles useful V1 substance; use <code>docs/v1-reconciliation-ledger.json</code> as the active status record and never treat Git recoverability as proof that migration is complete.

READMEs are living operational orientation. They should summarize the local contract and point to deeper BITwiki standards rather than duplicate entire architecture pages.

== BITwiki administration ==

Public project guidance belongs in the BITwiki namespace rather than subject Domains. Internal administrative records and maintenance procedures remain outside public wiki articles. Relevant public subjects include:

* architecture and ontology;
* programmable substrate and Lua modules;
* navigation and categories;
* epistemics/provenance;
* validation/quality;
* public guidance for identifying coverage gaps, without exposing an internal work queue;
* evidence-based improvement of navigation and contributor experience;
* governance/security;
* templates/properties/automation;
* BIThub/Discourse interoperability;
* source provenance and historical continuity, where they matter to readers.

== External lessons ==

Wikipedia's contribution/newcomer systems are useful evidence because they separate small improvements, help/tutorials, first-article creation, requested articles, drafts/review, and maintenance work. BITwiki should adopt the clarity of those pathways without copying their procedural sprawl.

Useful comparative sources include [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Contributing_to_Wikipedia Wikipedia's contribution guide], [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Your_first_article its first-article help], and [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Articles_for_creation its draft-review workflow]. These inform BITwiki's contributor navigation; they do not set BITwiki permissions or editorial standards. See [[BITwiki:Editorial protocol]] and [[BITwiki:Human-AI symbiosis]] for our own process and the historical human–AI collaboration model.

